WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Saunas are having a moment. Infrared cabins,
sauna blankets, thousand-dollar wellness gadgets,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.120
and the claims are everywhere. Detox,
heart health, even a longer life.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.160
But what does 20 years of actual research prove?

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.480
And which popular claim has no evidence at all?
Stick around. The honest answer surprised me.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:26.560
First, some history. Sauna bathing goes back
roughly 2,000 years in Finland, where wood-fired

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.600
saunas were a weekly ritual. Part hygiene,
part social, part something close to sacred.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:38.480
The traditional Finnish sauna heats the air
itself to somewhere between 80 and 100°C, about

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:46.640
175 to 212°F, with low humidity. You sit in the
heat for 15 to 20 minutes, cool off, and repeat.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.280
Infrared saunas work differently. Instead of
heating the air around you, infrared panels emit

00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:00.800
light that warms your body directly, so the air
stays much cooler, typically 120 to 170°F.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.800
That's why people who find traditional saunas
stifling often tolerate infrared. Most home

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.600
cabins, and all of those sauna blankets, use far
infrared, one slice of the infrared spectrum.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.280
And here's the first thing to be honest about.
Almost everything impressive you're about to

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:22.000
hear was studied in traditional Finnish saunas,
not infrared ones. Keep that in your back pocket.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.320
The strongest evidence in all of sauna science
comes from Finland. In 2015, researchers at the

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.200
University of Eastern Finland published a
landmark study in JAMA Internal Medicine.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:40.480
They followed 2,315 middle-aged Finnish men
for a median of nearly 21 years, tracking

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:46.600
sauna frequency against four outcomes. Sudden
cardiac death, fatal coronary heart disease,

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:52.800
fatal cardiovascular disease, and death from any
cause. Compared with men who sauned once a week,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.560
those who went four to seven times a week
had a 63% lower risk of sudden cardiac death,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.800
a 50% lower risk of fatal cardiovascular disease,
and a 40% lower risk of dying from any cause.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:11.240
Longer sessions help, too. Over 19 minutes
beat under 11. Now, the crucial part.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:16.800
This was an observational study. It shows a
strong association with a clean dose-response.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.200
More sauna, lower risk. And the researchers
adjusted for fitness, smoking, blood pressure,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.800
and more. But, association is not proof.
It does not prove saunas cause longer life.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.480
Two years later, the same Finnish cohort produced
another striking result, published in the journal

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:40.960
Age and Ageing. Men who used a sauna four to seven
times a week had a 66% lower risk of developing

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:46.680
dementia and a 65% lower risk of Alzheimer's
disease compared with once-a-week users.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.920
A larger follow-up in 2020, covering nearly 14,000
Finnish adults over up to 39 years, pointed the

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.120
same direction. Though its authors were notably
restrained, saying no firm conclusions could be

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.760
drawn yet. Plausible mechanisms exist. Heat
drives heart rate to exercise-like levels

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:10.320
and boosts blood flow, including to the
brain. But again, association, not proof.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.480
So, where does infrared itself have real
trial data? The best example is Waon therapy,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:25.760
developed in Japan. A far infrared dry
sauna at 60°C, about 140°F, for 15 minutes,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:32.240
followed by 30 minutes of rest under a blanket.
In a 2008 randomized trial of 188 patients with

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.680
chronic heart failure, two weeks of daily Waon
sessions improved the heart's pumping function

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.800
and lowered a blood marker of heart strain
compared with standard care alone. A larger

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.720
multicenter trial in 2016 found similar safety
and functional benefits in advanced heart failure.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:55.280
But, context matters enormously. These were sick
patients in clinics under medical supervision.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.320
Waon was an addition to standard
treatment, not a replacement.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.240
This is not evidence that a home infrared
cabin does the same thing for a healthy person.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:12.840
And now the honest part, the payoff I promised.
A 2023 randomized trial put 41 people with stable

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:18.000
heart disease through eight weeks of traditional
Finnish sauna four times a week and found no

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.200
improvement in a key measure of blood vessel
function versus controls. The big observational

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.560
numbers don't automatically transfer. As for
infrared specifically, the evidence is thin.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.520
Small trials suggest possible benefits for chronic
pain. There are early cardiovascular signals,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.520
but there is no long-term mortality data
for infrared at all. Nobody has run a

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.600
20-year infrared study. So when a brand cites the
Finnish research to sell you an infrared blanket,

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.120
they're borrowing evidence from a different kind
of sauna. That's the claim with no evidence.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:56.390
And if a brand claims ultra-low EMF, look for an
actual third-party lab report with a name on it,

00:04:56.390 --> 00:05:02.110
not just marketing copy. And one more time,
spoken plainly, this video is educational.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:07.310
It is not medical advice. If you have a heart
condition, low blood pressure, or you're pregnant,

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:13.910
talk to your doctor first. So here's the honest
summary. The strongest sauna science we have,

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:18.710
20 years of Finnish data on heart and brain
health, comes from traditional hot air saunas and

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:24.710
it's observational, compelling, dose-responsive,
but not proof. Infrared has genuine clinical trial

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:30.070
data in heart failure through Waon therapy, but
that's a medical setting, not your living room.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:35.310
And for at-home infrared wellness claims, the
evidence is still thin. If this kind of no hype

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:41.390
breakdown is useful, subscribe. This is episode
one of what the science actually says. Next time,

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:47.110
red light therapy, miracle or marketing? We'll
look at the actual studies. See you there.
